WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.000
What volume measures. Stock trading volume is the number of shares traded over a chosen period. It does not count unique investors.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Match the periods. An hourly volume bar counts activity for that hour. Compare bars using the same length of time.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Price and activity. A price move with high volume shows many shares traded. The bar alone does not explain why traders acted.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:28.000
A low-volume example. A price can move while few shares trade. Do not assume that a quiet bar means the price will reverse.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Read the labels. Check whether the chart reports shares, contracts, or another measure. Check whether the period is complete.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:12.000
News can shift interest. Company announcements and broad market events may change both price and volume. Verify the actual event.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Limits of a chart. Historical price and volume do not reveal every investor’s reason or guarantee a future direction.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Use a careful summary. Describe what changed, the timeframe, and the volume shown. Then separately note what remains unknown.
